          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 2,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "True or False?",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "grid",
    "columns": 2,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "If two angles and a side are given, the sine rule can be used to find the remaining sides.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Find the third angle using the triangle angle sum. The known side and its opposite angle form a complete pair, so the sine rule gives each remaining side.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "If two sides and an angle are given, the sine rule can be used to find the remaining sides.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False. The given angle must be opposite one of the given sides.",
          "worked": "False. Two sides and their included angle do not provide a complete opposite angle–side pair. The sine rule cannot be applied directly in that case.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "The ambiguous case arises when solving for lengths using the sine rule.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. The ambiguous case arises when solving for an angle because $\\sin\\theta=\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "In scenarios where the ambiguous case arises, it is possible for the ambiguous case to be invalid.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "True. Reject the supplementary candidate if it and the given angle sum to $180^\\circ$ or more, since the third angle must be positive.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "When solving a sine-rule equation for an unknown angle, any distinct supplementary candidate must be checked against the other givens and the triangle angle sum.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "True.",
          "worked": "When solving a sine-rule equation for an unknown angle, check the supplementary candidate against the other givens and the triangle angle sum. A second valid triangle is not guaranteed.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      },
      {
        "id": "p24-cc1-6-f",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "f",
        "prompt": "The ambiguous case is always valid when one of the angles of the triangle is obtuse.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "False.",
          "worked": "False. An obtuse angle does not guarantee a second valid triangle. Every candidate must satisfy the givens and leave a positive third angle.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 6
      }
    ]
  },
